  "entries": {
    "box": {
      "word": "BOX",
      "definitions": [
        {
          "text": "A tree or shrub, flourishing in different parts of the world. The common box (Buxus sempervirens) has two varieties, one of which, the dwaft box (B.suffruticosa), is much used for borders in gardens. The wood of the tree varieties, being very hard and smooth, is extensively used in the arts, as by turners, engravers, mathematical instrument makers, etc. Box elder, the ash-leaved maple (Negundo aceroides), of North America.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "A receptacle or case of any firm material and of various shapes.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "The quantity that a box contain.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "A space with a few seats partitioned off in a theater, or other place of public amusement. Laughed at by the pit, box, galleries, nay, stage. Dorset. The boxes and the pit are sovereign judges. Dryden.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "A chest or any receptacle for the deposit of money; as, a poor box; a contribution box. Yet since his neighbors give, the churl unlocks, Damning the poor, his tripple-bolted box. J. Warton.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "A small country house. \"A shooting box.\" Wilson. Tight boxes neatly sashed. Cowper.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "A boxlike shed for shelter; as, a sentry box.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "(Mach) (a) An axle box, journal box, journal bearing, or bushing. (b) A chamber or section of tube in which a valve works; the bucket of a lifting pump.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "The driver's seat on a carriage or coach.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "A present in a box; a present; esp. a Christmas box or gift. \"A Christmas box.\" Dickens.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "(Baseball)",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "The square in which the pitcher stands.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "(Zoöl.)",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "A Mediterranean food fish; the bogue. maker, box circle, etc.; also with modifying substantives; as money box, letter box, bandbox, hatbox or hat box, snuff box or snuffbox. Box beam (Arch.), a beam made of metal plates so as to have the form of a long box. inclosed on the sides to protect its contents. preserve its proper position. cape to carry off the rain. parts in machinery. rest with the legs retracted, resembles a box. with flat top and bottom. formed by a collar on one roll fitting between collars on another. R. W. Raymond. antimony, for the bearings of journals, etc. the genera Cistudo and Emys; -- so named because it can withdraw entirely within its shell, which can be closed by hinged joints in the lower shell. Also, humorously, an exceedingly reticent person. Emerson. difficulty. (Colloq.) -- In the wrong box, out of one's place; out of one's element; awkwardly situated. (Colloq.) Ridley (1554)",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "To inclose in a box.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To furnish with boxes, as a wheel.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "(Arch.)",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To inclose with boarding, lathing, etc., so as to bring to a required form. To box a tree, to make an incision or hole in a tree for the purpose of procuring the sap. boxed up twelve score pounds. (b) To confine; as, to be boxed up in narrow quarters.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "A blow on the head or ear with the hand. A good-humored box on the ear. W. Irving.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "To fight with the fist; to combat with, or as with, the hand or fist; to spar.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To strike with the hand or fist, especially to strike on the ear, or on the side of the head.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To boxhaul. To box off (Naut.), to turn the head of a vessel either way by bracing the headyards aback. compass in their order.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        }
      ],
